This file exists to make bootstrap/cache SURVIVE the deploy.

WHY A FILE AND NOT JUST THE DIRECTORY

Laravel gitignores everything inside `bootstrap/cache`, and the FTP deploy excludes `.git` files.
Between those two facts the directory itself is never transferred, because git does not track an
empty directory and there was no non-ignored file inside it to carry it.

That is not a cosmetic gap. `artisan` bootstraps through `bootstrap/app.php`, which requires this
directory for its package manifest. Absent, PHP dies with a fatal BEFORE Laravel's error handler is
registered, so the browser receives **500 with a zero-length body** and NOTHING is written to
`storage/logs`. There is no message anywhere to search for.

WHY IT CANNOT BE FIXED ONLY IN CODE

The obvious fix -- create the directory in `itrequest:install` or `itrequest:deploy` -- DOES NOT WORK
ALONE, and this was verified rather than assumed. Those commands are Laravel commands, and Laravel
cannot boot without the directory they are supposed to create. `artisan` never resolves the command,
so `ensureStorageTree()` is never reached. The dependency runs the wrong way.

THAT IS NOW FIXED, BUT NOT BY THESE FILES -- AND THIS SENTINEL IS STILL REQUIRED

`bootstrap/ensure-storage.php` is the file that actually repairs a fresh server. It is required by
BOTH `public/index.php` and `artisan`, before anything else is loaded, and it creates this directory
as well as the `storage/` tree. The same trap applies to `storage/framework/views`, which is excluded
from the transfer outright, and which is why the repair had to move out of the commands entirely.

This sentinel is kept anyway, and the reason is ordering. `ensure-storage.php` runs at the top of
`artisan` and `public/index.php`; it does NOT run for anything that loads `bootstrap/app.php`
directly, and it does not help if the deploy ever stops shipping that file. It costs 500 bytes of
comment and it makes the directory exist in git, which is the cheapest possible version of this
fix. Defence in depth on a host with no shell is not redundancy -- it is the only recovery path.

WHY THIS FILE IS SAFE IN A PUBLIC REPOSITORY

It contains no secrets and no configuration. It is a comment whose only functional purpose is to
make the directory exist in git, and therefore in the transfer.

The name is deliberate: `keep` is not matched by any pattern in the deploy's exclude list, unlike
`.gitignore`, which is matched by the `.git` wildcard rule. A `.gitkeep` would have been the
conventional choice and would have been excluded by the same rule that caused this.
